    {
      "id": "s05",
      "type": "DSA_TRACE_DP",
      "narration": "Every dynamic programming problem needs two things, and finding them is genuinely the whole job. First the base cases: the smallest problems you know without thinking. One stair has one way. Two stairs has two ways, two singles or one double. Those are given, not derived. Second the recurrence: how a bigger answer is built from smaller ones. To land on stair n you came from n minus one or from n minus two. So the ways to reach n are those two added, and that sentence is what you hunt for every time.",
      "durationFrames": 965,
      "timingSource": "tts",
