      "id": "s11",
      "type": "DSA_COST",
      "narration": "Here's what dynamic programming saves you from. Written as plain recursion, stair five asks for four and three, stair four asks for three and two, so stair three gets computed twice before we've properly started. Stair ten costs about a hundred and eighty calls. Stair forty costs over three hundred million, nearly all recomputing answers the program already had. The loop does forty additions. Same recurrence either way, because the only difference is whether you write down what you worked out.",
